# agent-session board-driver configuration.
#
# Copy to `.env`. The driver reads it from the current working directory.

REPO=owner/name
REPO_PATH=/absolute/path/to/target/repo
SKILL_DIR=/absolute/path/to/skills/agent-session
BOARD=owner/9

# --- GitHub credentials (issue #191) ----------------------------------------
#
# The agent gets a READ-SCOPED token; the driver performs every GitHub write.
# This is the containment layer: an agent holding a read-only token cannot merge,
# cannot rewrite an issue body and cannot force-push, whatever it decides to run.
# The prompt wording and the PreToolUse merge-block hook are defence in depth
# behind it, not a substitute for it.
#
# AGENT_GH_READ_TOKEN: a fine-grained PAT with read-only access to the target
# repo -- Contents, Issues, Pull requests and Metadata, all Read. Nothing else.
# Safe to keep here: the agent can read this file, and read is all it grants.
AGENT_GH_READ_TOKEN=github_pat_read_only_...

# DRIVER_GH_WRITE_TOKEN: DO NOT PUT THIS HERE.
#
# The agent has Read and Bash over REPO_PATH. If this file lives inside that tree
# -- which it does whenever the driver runs against its own repo -- then a write
# token written here is a write token the agent can just `cat`. The driver refuses
# to start in that case rather than ship the appearance of containment.
#
# Leave it unset and let the driver use the host `gh` keyring (`gh auth login`),
# or export it in the shell that launches the driver:
#
#   export DRIVER_GH_WRITE_TOKEN=github_pat_write_...
#
# With no AGENT_GH_READ_TOKEN configured at all, the loop still runs -- exactly as
# it did before #191, with the agent inheriting the host's write-capable auth --
# and prints a warning at startup saying so.

# --- models and budget ------------------------------------------------------

HIGH_TIER_MODEL=
LOW_TIER_MODEL=
MAX_BUDGET_USD=10.0
MAX_ISSUES=1
